Current Experiments
Blind versus reference under best-of-n
Question: Does giving a judge a reference cause a real downstream failure when we use that judge to select the best answer?
For each problem, generate a pool of candidates, e.g. 64 answers, and use the independent ground-truth checker to know which are actually correct.
Have the same judge score all 64 candidates blind, and separately score all 64 with one valid reference answer shown.
In each condition, choose the judge's highest-scoring answer and compare what happens: Does the reference increase score inflation, make the judge select reference-matching answers, or hurt true selection quality.
Maybe multiple runs picking the various valid answers as the reference to ensure the effect is robust (a CI if you will). 
Anchoring versus weakness
Question: Does reference anchoring remain even when the judge has already proven it is capable of recognizing the alternative solution as correct?
For every judge, show it two genuinely different valid answers without a reference and independently check that the judge correctly recognizes both as valid. Keep only those cases.
Now show that same judge the same answers again, but designate one of them as the reference.
Measure whether the judge now gives the reference-matching answer a higher score than the alternative answer it had already demonstrated it knew was correct.
Reference rotation
Question: If I change only which valid answer is shown as the reference, does the judge’s score of the exact same candidate change?
Start with two genuinely different valid answers, A and B. Use an independent oracle so we know both are correct.
Establish blind competence first. Make sure that this exact judge recognizes both A and B as correct when no reference is shown.
Rotation 1: show A as the reference and score both A and B.
Rotation 2: show B as the reference and score the exact same A and B.
Measure whether preference flips: does A receive the premium when A is the reference, and B receive the premium when B is the reference?
Repeat this across strong 32B–70B-class judges, multiple model families, and several benchmark/task types.
When does anchoring become exploitable?
Question: Does reference anchoring cause downstream failure specifically when the candidate pool contains an answer that matches the shown reference?
For each multi-valid problem, choose one valid answer A as the reference and identify other equally valid answers B, C, ....
Construct matched candidate pools where everything is kept as similar as possible except whether a reference-matching answer A is available: e.g. Pool 1 has no A; Pool 2 contains A.
Have the same judge score each pool blind and with A shown as the reference, then perform the same best-of-n selection.
Compare whether the referenced judge begins selecting A disproportionately once A becomes available, and whether this increases inflation or harms selection relative to the blind judge.
Optionally extend from 0 vs. 1 matching opportunity to 0,1,2,4,… reference-matching candidates to see whether greater opportunity makes the failure easier to exploit.
Show the missing link between reference anchoring and downstream optimization failure: the judge has a reference-induced preference, and best-of-n fails when the candidate pool gives the optimizer an opportunity to exploit that preference.
Distance from the reference
Question: Does the judge penalize a valid answer more as that answer becomes more different from the shown reference?
For each problem, obtain several genuinely different valid answers, ideally representing different solution types/approaches.
Choose one valid answer A as the reference.
Score all the valid alternatives blind and then with A shown as the reference.
Group the valid alternatives by how different they are from A—for example, same approach expressed differently versus a genuinely different solution strategy.
Test whether the reference-induced score penalty grows as valid solutions become more different from the shown reference.
Rotate which valid answer is the reference and repeat, so the “favored region” of the answer space should move with the reference.
Goal: Show that the judge is not merely biased toward one answer; it behaves as though correctness is partly being replaced by proximity to the arbitrary reference.
Can reference similarity overpower correctness?
Question: Can showing a reference make the judge prefer an incorrect but reference-like candidate over a correct candidate that uses a different solution?
Choose a specific question and set it as the reference
Construct a valid-near candidate. Correct. Similar to the reference in structure or method. Not necessarily an exact duplicate.
Construct a valid-far candidate B. Correct according to the same oracle. Uses a different algorithm, proof strategy, or structural solution. Match approximate length, polish, and explanatory quality to the other candidates.
Construct an invalid-near candidate C. Start from the reference or a near-reference solution. Introduce one subtle but genuine error. Confirm failure with the oracle.
Construct an invalid-far candidate D. Incorrect. Structurally unrelated to the reference. Similar in length and presentation.
Verify all four candidates independently.
A and B must pass.
C and D must fail.
Have an independent human or model audit the naturalness of the candidate construction without seeing judge scores.
Do not select errors based on which ones happen to fool the target judge.
Run the blind condition.
Score all four candidates.
Pairwise compare B versus C.
Reverse their order.
Include a tie option where appropriate.
Run the referenced condition with A shown.
Strong result: The reference can make similarity outweigh correctness.
